NNAAMMEE
     ppwwdd — return working directory name

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     ppwwdd [--LLPP]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The ppwwdd utility prints the absolute pathname of the current working di‐
     rectory to the standard output.

     The options are as follows:

     --LL      If the PWD environment variable is an absolute pathname that ref‐
             erences the current directory and doesn't contain path components
             of "." or "..", then PWD is printed as the name of the current
             directory.  Otherwise, act as if the --PP option was given.

     --PP      Print the physical path to the current working directory, with
             symbolic links in the path resolved.

     The --LL and --PP options override each other and the command's actions are
     determined by the last one specified.  The default if no options are
     given is --PP.

     For an explanation of the directory hierarchy, see hier(7).

EENNVVIIRROONNMMEENNTT
     PWD  The presumptive logical path to the current working directory.

EEXXIITT SSTTAATTUUSS
     The ppwwdd utility exits 0 on success, and >0 if an error occurs.

SSEEEE AALLSSOO
     ksh(1), getcwd(3), hier(7)

SSTTAANNDDAARRDDSS
     The ppwwdd utility is compliant with the IEEE Std 1003.1-2008 (“POSIX.1”)
     specification, except the standard says the default if no options are
     given should be to behave as if --LL had been specified.

HHIISSTTOORRYY
     An ppwwdd utility appeared in Version 5 AT&T UNIX.  Support for the --LL op‐
     tion was added in OpenBSD 5.6.

CCAAVVEEAATTSS
     ppwwdd also exists as a built-in to ksh(1), which may have a different de‐
     fault behavior.

